# [Non-decreasing Subsequences](https://leetcode.com/problems/non-decreasing-subsequences/)

## Approach: Backtracking + Set

- Use recursion to explore two choices for each element: include it or skip it.
- Include an element only if the subsequence remains non-decreasing.
- Store subsequences of length greater than one in a `set<vector<int>>` to eliminate duplicates.
- Copy the unique subsequences into the answer vector.

## Code

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    set<vector<int>> st;

    void find_Subsequences(vector<int>& nums, vector<int>& temp,
                           int curr, int prev) {
        if (curr >= nums.size()) {
            if (temp.size() > 1) st.insert(temp);
            return;
        }

        if (prev == -1 || nums[curr] >= nums[prev]) {
            temp.push_back(nums[curr]);
            find_Subsequences(nums, temp, curr + 1, curr);
            temp.pop_back();
        }

        find_Subsequences(nums, temp, curr + 1, prev);
    }

    vector<vector<int>> findSubsequences(vector<int>& nums) {
        vector<int> temp;
        vector<vector<int>> ans;

        st.clear();
        find_Subsequences(nums, temp, 0, -1);

        for (const auto& it : st) {
            ans.push_back(it);
        }

        return ans;
    }
};
```

## Complexity Analysis

Let \(n\) be the length of `nums`.

- **Time Complexity:** Approximately \(O(2^n \cdot n \log(2^n))\) as an upper bound for generating subsequences and inserting them into the set.
- **Space Complexity:** \(O(2^n \cdot n)\) for storing unique subsequences, with \(O(n)\) additional recursion and temporary-vector space.

The set-based approach is correct, though a local `unordered_set<int>` at each recursion level can avoid duplicate branches and eliminate the need for a global set.
